<Guide lang="en" slug="building-on-your-own-land-outdoor-kitchen" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="renovatie" ctaTitle="Want an outdoor kitchen built?" ctaText="Tell us where it should go and what you want in it. We design it and build it, including water and electricity.">
  <h2 id="allowed">Is an outdoor kitchen allowed?</h2>
  <p>With a detached house, usually yes. How you arrange it depends on what you build:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Freestanding barbecue or mobile outdoor kitchen</strong>: no permit needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Masonry outdoor kitchen without a roof</strong>: often a notification (<em>declaración responsable</em>) to the town hall.</li>
    <li><strong>Outdoor kitchen with a fixed canopy</strong>: the canopy usually needs a permit and counts towards the building allowance. See <a href="/en/guides/building-on-your-own-land-spain/carport-and-pergola/">carport, canopy and pergola</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On a finca (<em>suelo rústico</em>) the same steps apply, but a canopy is only allowed for a legal house with building allowance left. In an apartment complex everything depends on the community: read <a href="/en/guides/building-on-your-own-land-spain/barbecue-in-apartments/">barbecues in apartment complexes</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="fire">Fire season: barbecue ban</h2>
  <p>Many new owners overlook this. From <strong>1 June to 15 October</strong> Andalusia is in its high fire-risk period. During that time, lighting fires is banned on forest and natural land (<em>terreno forestal</em>) and in the <strong>400-metre</strong> strip around it (<em>zona de influencia forestal</em>). That includes barbecues. In dry years the Junta de Andalucía can extend the period.</p>
  <p>In the Axarquía hills many fincas lie in or close to such a zone. Ask the town hall whether your plot is in it and what applies to a fixed barbecue at your house. An outdoor kitchen with a gas or electric grill next to the charcoal barbecue makes you less dependent on the season.</p>

  <h2 id="options">What are your options?</h2>
  <table class="prices plain">
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Freestanding gas or charcoal barbecue</td><td>Not a structure, can be moved.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Masonry block with barbecue and worktop</td><td>The classic Andalusian <em>barbacoa de obra</em>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Complete outdoor kitchen with sink, power and fridge</td><td>Laying water and drainage is often the bulk of the work.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>With pizza oven, natural stone and lighting</td><td>For those who cook and entertain outdoors a lot.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>With fixed canopy or louvred roof</td><td>Usable in blazing sun and rain. Usually needs a permit.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Want this built? Tell us what you have in mind. We find out what is allowed, arrange the permit and build it for you.</strong> <a href="/en/request/?dienst=renovatie">Get in touch →</a></p>

  <h2 id="tips">What to watch out for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Materials</strong>: sun, salt near the coast and winter rain. Choose stainless steel, natural stone or ceramics, not ordinary wood or chipboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Drainage</strong>: on a finca with a septic tank, the waste water must connect to it properly. Grease and food scraps do not belong in it.</li>
    <li><strong>Position and wind</strong>: place the barbecue so the smoke does not blow towards the house, the pool or the neighbours, and not under an overhanging roof or trees.</li>
    <li><strong>Electricity</strong>: outdoor sockets must be waterproof and separately protected, installed by a registered electrician.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Back to the overview: <a href="/en/guides/building-on-your-own-land-spain/">building on your own land</a>.</p>
</Guide>
